 this sounds bad and it is but economists  generally agree that it's better than  the alternative which is allowing prices  to continue rising at a fast pace that's  because inflation unchecked increases to  the point that a country's currency  loses so much value that it becomes  virtually worthless we've seen this  happen in places like germany and  zimbabwe and more recently in venezuela  the cost for everything jumps companies  can't buy materials or basic supplies  and workers lose their jobs when we last  saw inflation as high as it is now then  fed chairman paul volcker had interest  rates set at more than 14  and that was down from 19 just a few  months earlier that eventually ground  inflation back to a more manageable  level but it also brought about a  painful recession tanked the stock  market and sent u.s consumer sentiment  to its lowest point on record the  question many in the market are starting  to ask themselves is  will fed chair jerome powell be willing  to do the same thing today if necessary  there are worries that the answer is no  because of what we've seen from the  chairman in recent history powell said  for about a year that the current  inflation we're seeing would be  transitory and would recede on its own  before retiring the term in november and  committing to take action investors also  remember how powell reversed course on  raising interest rates in 2019 after the  stock market started to buckle interest  rates had reached just under 2.5 percent  at that point having been near zero for  more than a decade then there was the  unprecedented action pal and the fed  took in march 2020 that sent stocks  soaring even as the u.s economy had its  worst quarter in history that gave some  like alliance chief economic adviser  muhammad al-arian the impression that  this fed was doing more for the market  than the real economy so many are  watching every word from pow in the fed  to see if the policy makers there are  committed to doing whatever it takes to  tame inflation the fed will have to walk  a tightrope going forward if inflation  does ultimately cool later in the year  as they are again predicting raising  rates too fast could cause a recession  unnecessarily but if they raise rates  too slowly inflation could spike and get  out of control causing even greater pain  with so much uncertainty in the world  the only thing we know for sure is that  policymakers will need to be on their  a-game  if you enjoyed this video i want you to  hit that like button and subscribe to  keep up with my latest videos on markets  i want you to be like yo gotti who told  us in 2020 i'm a real hustler i'm  recession proof i know how to make the  paper and stack it too i'll see you next  time  you
 

